 ## Overview
-Vector-Matrix (VM) decomposition is a tensor factorization technique that relaxes the low-rank constraints for two modes of a tensor by factorizing it into compact vector and matrix factors, effectively acting as a special case of Block Term Decomposition (BTD) [S1][S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [CP Decomposition](2255-cp-decomposition.md), which uses a sum of outer products of pure vectors, VM uses a sum of vector-matrix outer products [S1][S2, unofficial]. This allows each component to express more complex tensor data of higher ranks, significantly reducing the required number of components compared to CP while maintaining a memory complexity of $O(N^2)$ compared to the $O(N^3)$ of dense grids [S1][S3, unofficial].
