 ## Subtopics
 
-### CP Decomposition
-The parent method using pure vector outer products. Choose CP when maximum compactness is required and the scene complexity is low enough to be represented by rank-one components. See [CP Decomposition](2255-cp-decomposition.md).
-
-### Block Term Decomposition (BTD)
-The general family of decompositions where blocks of arbitrary rank are summed. VM is a specific case of BTD where each block is a rank-one vector outer producted with a full-rank matrix. See [Block Term Decomposition](link-to-btd-if-available).
+### Balanced Spherical Feature Grid Decomposition
+An application of VM decomposition to the density feature grid $G_\sigma$ and appearance feature grid $G_a$, which are composed of two overset tensors (Yin and Yang) [S4]. The appearance grid $G_a$ is a 4D tensor $\mathbb{R}^{2N_y^r \times N_y^\theta \times N_y^\phi \times C}$. For the appearance feature mode ($C$), only vector components $\mathbf{b}$ are assigned instead of matrix components [S4]. The decomposition for the appearance grid is given by [S4]:
+$$
+\mathcal{G}_a^y = \sum_{n=1}^{N_a} \mathbf{v}_{a,n}^{y,R} \otimes \mathbf{M}_{a,n}^{y,\Theta\Phi} \otimes \mathbf{b}_{3n-2}^y + \mathbf{v}_{a,n}^{y,\Theta} \otimes \mathbf{M}_{a,n}^{y,\Phi R} \otimes \mathbf{b}_{3n-1}^y + \mathbf{v}_{a,n}^{y,\Phi} \otimes \mathbf{M}_{a,n}^{y,R\Theta} \otimes \mathbf{b}_{3n}^y
+$$
+where $\mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times 6N_a}$ is a matrix obtained by stacking all $\mathbf{b}^y$ vectors columnwise [S4]. The number of decomposed components for the density grid is typically $N_\sigma = 16$ [S4].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Reconstruction time is significantly faster than NeRF. TensoRF with VM decomposition achieves reconstruction in less than 10 minutes, which is about 100x faster than NeRF [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Reduces space complexity from $O(N^3)$ (dense voxel grid) to $O(N^2)$ [S1][S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Model Size**: Retains a compact model size of less than 75 MB for typical scenes [S1][S3, unofficial]. Note that VM increases the model size per component compared to CP, but significantly reduces the required number of components $R$ for the same expression capacity [S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Higher expressivity than CP; faster reconstruction than NeRF; lower memory footprint than dense grids.
-*   **Limitations**: Larger model size per component than CP; requires careful tuning of $R$ if scene complexity varies significantly across axes.
